%I A401224 #7 Oct 22 2026 21:49:52
%S A401224 1,32,224,316,1810,26,292,2118,13332,75398,402656,2037848,9894016,
%T A401224 46432230,211990020,945890248,4138161480,17797763512,75413531108,
%U A401224 315374655654,1303545782496,5331793888212,21602963350096,86781523032382,345894444930480,1368823914353928
%N A401224 Number of largest sets S of points of the 4 X n grid such that any two distinct rectangles with all four corners at grid points (tilted rectangles included) have distinct sets of corners outside S.
%C A401224 Sets are counted as subsets of the grid, not up to symmetry. A rectangle with all four corners in S would have the empty set of corners outside S, but for n >= 4 no set counted here contains all four corners of a rectangle.
%C A401224 a(n) = A399580(4,n) for n >= 2. The largest size is n+3 for n <= 5 and n+4 for n >= 6 (see A397315), which is why a(6) = 26 is smaller than a(5) = 1810.
%C A401224 For n >= 6 every such set has four columns with two points each and one point in every other column, and the four row pairs are the edges of a 4-cycle on the four rows.
%C A401224 For n >= 6 the terms come from a finite automaton that reads a set column by column (see the Pemmasani link). Two transfer counters on this automaton agree for n <= 1500, and a direct test of every candidate set agrees for n <= 10.
%H A401224 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="/A401224/b401224.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1500</a>
%H A401224 Peter Kagey, <a href="https://peterkagey.com/problems/001/">Open Problem Collection, Problem 001</a>.
%H A401224 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/blob/main/kagey-problems/problem001/jis/pemmasani.pdf">Maximum Blackouts That Keep Every Lattice Rectangle Identifiable</a>, preprint, 2026.
%H A401224 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/tree/main/kagey-problems/problem001">Solvers, exhaustive verification, and pre-registration ledger for Problem 001</a> (GitHub repository).
%H A401224 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="/A401224/a401224.txt">Coefficients of P and Q in the g.f.</a>
%F A401224 G.f.: x + 32*x^2 + 224*x^3 + 316*x^4 + 1810*x^5 + P(x)/Q(x), where P(x) = 26*x^6 - 826*x^7 + ... has degree 444 and Q(x) = 1 - 43*x + 865*x^2 - ... has degree 420 (see the coefficients link).
%F A401224 Q(x) = (1-x)^2*(1+x)^2*q5a(x)^3*q5b(x)^3*q6(x)^3*q11(x)^5*q17(x)^3*q19(x)^3*q20(x)^5*q21(x)^5, where q5a, q5b, q6, ..., q21 are irreducible over the integers, with degrees given by their indices and constant term 1 (see the coefficients link).
%F A401224 Sum_{j=0..420} q(j)*a(n-j) = 0 for n >= 445, where q(j) is the coefficient of x^j in Q(x).
%F A401224 a(n) ~ c*n^4*r^n, where r = 3.351996777310375881993697... is the largest real root of x^20 - 4*x^19 + 2*x^18 + x^17 - 3*x^16 + 5*x^15 + x^14 - 3*x^13 + 7*x^12 + 2*x^11 + 30*x^10 - 7*x^9 + 10*x^8 + 12*x^7 + 2*x^6 + 17*x^5 + 26*x^4 + 19*x^3 - 26*x^2 - 4*x + 8 and c = 0.0000816109707754112490336898...
%F A401224 a(n) <= 3*n*(n-1)*(n-2)*(n-3)*4^(n-4)*(53/64)^max(0, floor(n/4)-4) for n >= 6.
%e A401224 a(6) = 26. In 20 of the sets the row pairs of the two-point columns are {1,2}, {2,3}, {3,4}, {1,4}, and in the other 6 they are {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,4}, {3,4}. One of the latter (1 = in S, rows numbered from the top):
%e A401224   1 1 0 0 0 0
%e A401224   1 0 0 1 1 0
%e A401224   0 1 1 0 0 1
%e A401224   0 0 0 0 1 1
%Y A401224 Row 4 of A399580.
%Y A401224 Cf. A397315, A401223, A401225.
%K A401224 nonn,new
%O A401224 1,2
